  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1213 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery and objective analysis this afternoon show a mid-\nlevel high centered near the Red River, with the region positioned \nalong the northern periphery of the associated ridge. Meanwhile, an \namplifying trough over the eastern CONUS has brought northwesterly \nflow aloft and ushered CAA into the area. This, combined with \nwidespread cloud cover, has kept afternoon temperatures to the \n70s.\n\nA shortwave disturbance moving across the region has supported low-\nlevel moisture transport and weak WAA across central and eastern \nNebraska, resulting in scattered showers and PoPs of 30-50%. A few \nthunderstorms will also be possible, particularly across southeast \nNebraska, where weak but sufficient instability of 300-400 J/kg \nMUCAPE is present. Shower coverage will gradually decrease this \nevening, with most locations receiving a few hundredths of an\ninch of precipitation and localized pockets peaking around\n0.25\".\n\nShowers are expected to redevelop overnight as another shortwave \ndisturbance emerges from the Rockies and increases PoPs to 50-80%. A \nband of moderate rainfall should develop from central into southeast \nNebraska near the nose of a strengthening nocturnal low-level jet. \nSufficient instability will remain across southeast Nebraska for a \nfew embedded thunderstorms. Some training of showers and storms will \nbe possible in this area, with an environment supportive of \nefficient rainfall. PWAT values will approaching 2 inches (above the \n90th percentile of sounding climatology), while warm-cloud depths \nincrease to around 4 to 4.5 km. Rainfall totals across the southern \nportion of the forecast area could reach 1 to 1.5 inches. \nHydrologic concerns will need to be monitored, and WPC maintains\na Slight Risk for excessive rainfall. However, recent dry \nconditions and ongoing crop water usage should make flash-flood \nguidance difficult to exceed. Rainfall amounts will decrease \nfarther north, where showers should remain lighter and more \nscattered through the day. Persistent cloud cover will again \nhold highs in the 70s on Thursday. \n\nShowers will diminish Thursday afternoon before increasing again \nThursday evening and overnight as another shortwave disturbance \ncrosses the region. A renewed nocturnal low-level jet will support \nwidespread shower development, with POps of 60-90%. Efficient \nrainfall ingredients will be in place, leading to localized bands of \n0.75 to 1.25 inches depending on where the heaviest showers develop. \nPrecipitation chances will gradually decrease Friday morning and \nafternoon, with skies beginning to clear from west to east. High \ntemperatures should recover into the upper 70s and lower 80s.\n\nSaturday and Beyond... \n\nMid- to upper-level riding will amplify and expand northward into \nthe region this weekend, resulting in a warming trend. Temperatures \nat 850 mb are forecast to rise into the 25 to 30 degree C range and \nshould mix efficiently to the surface both days. Highs Saturday will \nclimb into the 90s, with temperatures approaching 100 degrees across \nportions of east-central Nebraska. Low-level moisture transport and \nevapotranspiration will push dewpoints into the 70s, resulting in \nmuggy conditions and heat index values near 100 degrees across much \nof the area. Temperatures will increase further Sunday, with \nwidespread highs in the upper 90s to around 100 degrees and peak \nheat index values of 104 to 109 degrees. A Heat Advisory may \neventually be needed as forecast confidence increase.\n\nShortwave disturbances are expected to round the ridge early next \nweek, gradually dampening it and allowing temperatures to ease. \nHighs should remain in the mid to upper 90s Monday before falling \ninto the upper 80s and lower 90s by Wednesday. Long-range \nguidance remains inconsistent regards the tracks of these \ndisturbances, with some solutions keeping them farther north and\nother bringing them through the local area. This uncertainty \nsupports lower confidence in forecasted highs and periodic \nprecipitation chances through the work week, with PoPs generally\nranging from 15- 30%.",
